### **Your Literature Review ← IASME Insights**

**Section 2.3: Cyber Essentials Assessment Process**

```
2.3.1 Certification Pathway

The Cyber Essentials scheme is delivered by IASME Consortium, the NCSC's 
official delivery partner (IASME, 2025). The scheme offers two 
certification levels:

1. Cyber Essentials (CE): Self-assessment questionnaire independently 
   marked by qualified assessors

2. Cyber Essentials Plus (CE+): Technical audit including hands-on testing 
   of systems

[Quote from Source 2 - IASME Website]:
"The answers must be signed-off by a board member or equivalent and the 
answers are then marked by an independent Assessor" (IASME, 2025).

This board-level accountability ensures organizational commitment but can 
be intimidating for SMEs unfamiliar with cybersecurity terminology...

2.3.2 Assessment Rigour and Pass Criteria

The CE Plus Test Specification (IASME, 2025) reveals the strict pass/fail 
criteria applied by assessors. The specification states:

[Quote from Source 2 - Test Spec v3.2, p.6]:
"Any single Fail means a Fail for the assessment as a whole — unless a 
special exception is stated in this test specification" (IASME, 2025, p.6).

This binary approach creates high stakes for applicants. Even minor non-
compliances can result in failure, requiring remediation and 
resubmission...

2.3.3 Common Failure Points

Analysis of the CE Plus test cases identifies five critical areas where 
failures commonly occur:

Test 1: Vulnerability Assessment
- Services with CVSS ≥7.0 vulnerabilities (automatic fail)
- Default passwords unchanged (automatic fail)

Test 2: Patch Management
- Critical/high-risk patches >14 days overdue (automatic fail)
[This justifies your Patch Tracking Template]

Test 4: Multi-Factor Authentication
- Cloud services without MFA (automatic fail, no exceptions)
[Quote]: "authentication to cloud services must always use MFA" 
(IASME, 2025, p.20)
[This justifies your MFA Implementation Guide]

Test 5: Account Separation
- Users with admin privileges for daily tasks (automatic fail)
[This justifies your User Access Control Matrix]

2.3.4 Financial and Temporal Costs

IASME's pricing structure (2025) scales by organization size:
- Micro (0-9 employees): £384 inc. VAT
- Small (10-49 employees): £528 inc. VAT
- Medium (50-249 employees): £600 inc. VAT
- Large (250+ employees): £720 inc. VAT

However, the true cost for unprepared organizations is higher. Failed 
assessments require:
- Remediation work (time/cost)
- Re-submission within 2 working days (time pressure)
- Potential consultant fees (£1,000-£5,000)
- Delayed access to contracts requiring CE

A readiness toolkit addressing the common failure points identified above 
could significantly reduce first-time failure rates...
